---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import { fieldDependents, whereUsed } from "../../data/page-answers.ts";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Where Is This Salesforce Field Used? Find Every Reference"
  description="Find every flow, Apex class, formula, layout and sharing rule that uses a Salesforce field, in one question, with a list of what wasn't checked."
  path="/use-cases/where-is-a-field-used"
  heading="Where is this field used?"
  lede="Instead of clicking through Setup one field at a time, ask once and get every flow, class, formula, layout and sharing rule that touches the field, each one named."
  crumb="Where a field is used"
  audience="admins and developers"
  short="Ask your AI assistant “where is the Status field on Invoice used?”. sf-intelligence looks through your org’s metadata snapshot and lists every component that reads, writes or depends on the field, says how each one was found, and names the kinds of metadata it couldn’t check, so an empty answer isn’t mistaken for “unused”."
  answers={[whereUsed, fieldDependents]}
  answerIntro="Two fields on Verdant, the demo org: one with a short answer, one used all over the org."
  asks={[
    "Where is the Status field on Invoice used?",
    "What uses Opportunity.Region__c?",
    "Which flows and classes touch the Amount field?",
    "Is anything still using Contact.Legacy_Status__c?",
  ]}
  checks={[
    "<strong>Apex classes and triggers</strong>, from the parsed source, plus a text search for anything the parser missed",
    "<strong>Flows</strong> that read or set the field, and the conditions that start them",
    "<strong>Formulas, roll-ups and validation rules</strong>",
    "<strong>Page layouts, sharing rules and analytics</strong> that reference it",
    "<strong>Lightning components and Visualforce</strong>, when your refresh pulled them",
  ]}
  limits={[
    "Field names put together at run time in Apex",
    "Reports beyond the ones your refresh pulled",
    "Managed packages, integrations and data loads",
    "Kinds of metadata your refresh skipped. Each answer names them",
  ]}
  tools={["find_component_usages", "field_360", "find_field_anywhere", "get_impact", "find_code_usages"]}
  related={[
    { href: "/use-cases/what-breaks-if-you-delete-a-field", label: "What breaks if I delete this field?", note: "the same check, with a verdict" },
    { href: "/use-cases/salesforce-dependency-analysis", label: "Dependency analysis", note: "for flows, classes and objects" },
    { href: "/blog/delete-unused-salesforce-fields", label: "Can I delete this Salesforce field?", note: "guide" },
    { href: "/blog/salesforce-data-dictionary", label: "How to create a Salesforce data dictionary", note: "guide" },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "How is this different from Salesforce’s “Where is this used?” button?",
      a: "The button covers many reference types, one field at a time, inside Setup. sf-intelligence answers from a snapshot of the whole org, in plain language, and adds a text search of the source for references the dependency data doesn’t record. It also tells you which kinds of metadata it couldn’t check.",
    },
    {
      q: "Does it find references inside Apex and formulas?",
      a: "Yes. It parses Apex and formula source and reports each reference with how it was found. Field names assembled at run time are invisible to any static check, so “nothing found” means “no evidence in the metadata”, not a guarantee.",
    },
    {
      q: "Does it work on standard fields?",
      a: "Yes. A normal refresh reads the standard fields of the core objects from your org, so you can ask the same way, for example “where is Account.Industry used?”.",
    },
  ]}
  cta={{ question: whereUsed.question, related: { href: "/use-cases/what-breaks-if-you-delete-a-field", label: "Before you delete a field" } }}
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <h2>Usage first, verdict second</h2>
  <p>“Where is it used?” and “can I delete it?” sound like the same question, but they aren’t. The first gives you a list. The second weighs that list and adds what wasn’t checked before it says yes or no. Start here when you’re exploring a field you didn’t build. Move to <a href="/use-cases/what-breaks-if-you-delete-a-field">what breaks if I delete it</a> when you’re about to change it.</p>
  <h2>Access isn’t usage</h2>
  <p>Profiles and permission sets that grant access to a field are listed separately from the components that use it. A permission set that can see a field doesn’t stop you deleting it. A flow that sets it does.</p>
</UseCaseLayout>
